        "math_academy_id": "q-184639",
        "problem": "Find ${\u22c3}_{i=0}^{2}{X}_{i}$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{0}={-2,-1,2},\\,{X}_{1}={-1,0,2},\\,{X}_{2}={-2,0,3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "The union of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n{\u22c3}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one\u00a0}{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nThe notation ${\u22c3}_{i=0}^{2}$ means that our index set is \n${0,1,2}.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u22c3}_{i=0}^{2}{X}_{i} & ={X}_{0}\\cup {X}_{1}\\cup {X}_{2} \\\\  & ={-2,-1,2}\\,\\cup \\,{-1,0,2}\\,\\cup \\,{-2,0,3} \\\\  & ={-2,-1,0,2,3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
